.TH GEMWRITE-CLI 1 "September 2026" "gemwrite" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gemwrite-cli \- edit GemWrite documents from the shell or a script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gemwrite-cli
.RI [ FILE ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]...
.RI [ COMMAND " [" ARGS ]...]...
.br
.B gemwrite-cli
.RI [ FILE ]
.B \-\-script
.I SCRIPT
.br
.B gemwrite-cli \-\-list
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gemwrite-cli
loads a document into memory, runs a sequence of editing and query commands
against it, and exits. It needs no display, so it works over SSH, in
containers, and from programs that drive an editor on a user's behalf.
.PP
It offers the same operations as the GemWrite window: most commands here
are also menu items and keyboard shortcuts in
.BR gemwrite ,
because both run the same command table.
.PP
Each invocation is independent. The caret starts at the beginning of the
document, the commands run in order, and the in-memory document is discarded
when the process exits. Nothing is written to disk unless a
.B save
or
.B save-as
command runs. Commands that need to build on each other (find something, then
format it) must therefore run in the same invocation.
.PP
Commands take a fixed number of arguments, so several can follow one another
on the command line without separators. A literal
.B \-\-
between commands is accepted and ignored for readability.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I FILE
Document to load before running commands: OpenDocument Text
.RB ( .odt )
by extension, otherwise UTF-8 plain text. If omitted, the run starts with an
empty untitled document, which
.B save-as
can write anywhere.
.TP
.BI \-\-script " SCRIPT"
Read commands from
.I SCRIPT
one line at a time, or from standard input when
.I SCRIPT
is
.BR \- .
See
.B SCRIPT MODE
below. Commands given on the command line run first.
.TP
.BI \-\-author " NAME"
Sign the comments and replies this run adds as
.IR NAME ,
instead of the
.B author
key in
.I ~/.config/gemwrite/settings.ini
or the account's name. Useful when a program comments on someone's behalf.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print every command with its arguments, grouped as below, and exit. This
is generated from the command table and is always complete.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
Arguments in capitals are required. Where a command says
.IR POS ,
see
.B POSITIONS
below.
.SS File
.TP
.BI open " FILE"
Discard the current document without saving and load
.IR FILE .
Useful only in a script that handles several files in one run.
.TP
.B save
Write the document back to the file it was loaded from. Fails on an untitled
document.
.TP
.BI save-as " FILE"
Write the document to
.I FILE
and make that the current file. The format follows the extension.
.TP
.B reload
Read the file again, dropping unsaved changes, as one undo step: the caret
stays where it was and
.B undo
brings the previous text back.
.TP
.BI export-pdf " FILE"
Write every page to
.I FILE
as a PDF, at the document's page size, as it would print: comments aren't
marked. Like
.BR export-markdown ,
this is a copy, not a save.
.TP
.BI export-markdown " FILE"
Write the document to
.I FILE
as Markdown. The document keeps its own file and its unsaved changes: this
is a copy, not a save. Title and Heading 1 become
.BR # ,
Heading 2 and 3
.B ##
and
.BR ### ,
Quote paragraphs a block quote, Note paragraphs a
.B "> [!NOTE]"
callout, list items
.B -
and
.B 1.
lines nested by four spaces, bold and italic asterisks, underline a
.B <u>
tag. Alignment, spacing, fonts and comments have no Markdown form and are
left out. Text that would read as Markdown syntax is backslash-escaped.
.SS Editing
.TP
.BR undo ", " redo
Step back or forward through the edit history. A run of typed characters at
one spot is a single step, as is a run of backspaces; paragraph breaks,
formatting changes, and replacements of a selection are their own steps.
Loading a file empties the history. Fails when there is nothing to undo or
redo.
.TP
.BR cut ", " copy ", " paste
Move or copy the selection to the clipboard, or insert the clipboard at the
caret in place of the selection. The clipboard keeps character formatting
and paragraph styles, lasts for the run, and is not the system clipboard.
.B cut
and
.B copy
fail without a selection;
.B paste
fails while the clipboard is empty.
.TP
.BI insert-text " TEXT"
Insert
.I TEXT
at the caret, replacing the selection if there is one. Newlines inside
.I TEXT
start new paragraphs.
.TP
.B new-paragraph
Split the paragraph at the caret, like pressing Enter.
.TP
.BR delete-backward ", " delete-forward
Delete the selection, or one character before or after the caret when nothing
is selected.
.TP
.B delete-selection
Delete the selection; does nothing without one.
.TP
.B select-all
Select the whole document.
.TP
.BI goto " POS"
Place the caret at
.I POS
and clear the selection.
.TP
.BI select " FROM TO"
Select from position
.I FROM
to position
.IR TO .
The caret ends at
.IR TO .
.TP
.BI move " UNIT COUNT"
Move the caret
.I COUNT
units; negative counts move backwards. See
.B UNITS
below.
.TP
.BI extend " UNIT COUNT"
Like
.B move
but grows the selection instead of moving the caret alone.
.TP
.BI find " TEXT"
Make
.I TEXT
the search term and select its first match after the current selection,
wrapping to the start once. Prints the match's position. Fails if there is
no match. Matching ignores case (see
.BR match-case ),
and a match never spans paragraphs.
.TP
.BR find-next ", " find-previous
Select the next match of the search term after the selection, or the
previous one before it, wrapping once. Prints the match's position.
.TP
.BI match-case " true|false"
Whether the search term must match case; off by default.
.TP
.BI replace " TEXT"
Replace the selected match of the search term with
.I TEXT
and select the next match. Fails when the selection is not a match. The
new text keeps the formatting of the first character it replaced.
.TP
.BI replace-all " FIND TEXT"
Make
.I FIND
the search term and replace every match with
.IR TEXT ,
as one undo step. Prints how many were replaced; fails if there were none.
.SS Formatting
.TP
.BR bold ", " italic ", " underline
Toggle the attribute on the selection, or on the next inserted text when
nothing is selected. With a selection, the new state is the opposite of the
first selected character.
.TP
.BI set-font " FAMILY"
Set the font family, for example
.B Serif
or
.BR "DejaVu Sans" .
.TP
.BI set-size " POINTS"
Set the font size in points, 4 to 400. Decimals are allowed.
.TP
.BR font-bigger ", " font-smaller
Step the size through the same list the toolbar uses.
.TP
.BI align " left" | center | right | justify
Set the alignment of every paragraph the selection touches.
.TP
.BI line-spacing " FACTOR"
Set the line spacing of every paragraph the selection touches, as a multiple
of the font's line height: 1 is single, 1.5 is one and a half, 2 is double.
Any value from 0.5 to 10 is accepted.
.TP
.BR spacing-single ", " spacing-1.5 ", " spacing-double
Shorthands for the common line spacings.
.TP
.BR indent ", " outdent
Move the left edge of every paragraph the selection touches in or out by
half an inch (36pt), never past the margin or so far that less than an
inch of the line is left; a list item nests or unnests instead. Going as
far as it goes is not an error.
.TP
.BR indent-left ", " indent-right ", " indent-first " " POINTS
Set one indent of every paragraph the selection touches, in points from
the margin (72 to an inch). The first-line indent is measured from the
left indent and may be negative for a hanging indent. Values are clamped so
that an inch of the line is left for text; a list item has no first-line
indent of its own, its label hangs instead.
.TP
.BI tab-add " POINTS TYPE"
Add a tab stop at a position measured from the left indent, of type
.BR left ", " center ", " right " or " decimal
(text lines up on its decimal point). A stop within half a point of an
existing one replaces it. Past a paragraph's last stop, tabs fall every
half inch.
.TP
.BI tab-remove " POINTS"
Remove the tab stop at that position; fails when none of the touched
paragraphs has one.
.TP
.B tab-clear
Remove every tab stop of the touched paragraphs.
.SS Paragraph Style
.TP
.BI set-style " NAME"
Apply a named paragraph style to every paragraph the selection touches. The
built-in names are
.BR Standard ", " Title ", " "Heading 1" ", " "Heading 2" ", " "Heading 3" ", " Quote " (an indented italic block quotation), and " Note " (a tinted box with a rule down its left edge);
.B styles
lists the names in the current document.
.TP
.BR style-body ", " style-title ", " style-h1 ", " style-h2 ", " style-h3 ", " style-quote ", " style-note
Shorthands for the built-in styles.
.SS Lists
Items of a list are paragraphs in the
.B List Bullet
or
.B List Number
style, at a level of nesting from 0 to 9. Numbers are counted, never typed:
they run along consecutive items at one level, start over after any other
paragraph, and nested levels count on their own (1. a. i.; bullets go
\(bu \(ci \(sq).
.TP
.BR list-bullet ", " list-number
Make every paragraph the selection touches an item of that kind, keeping the
level of any that already are items. When they all are that kind already,
they return to body text instead, so each command toggles.
.B indent
and
.B outdent
(see Formatting) nest an item one level deeper or step it back out;
outdenting a top-level item makes it body text.
.B new-paragraph
on an empty item and
.B delete-backward
at the start of one step out the same way, as Enter and Backspace do in the
window.
.SS Spelling
Checking uses enchant, so the dictionaries are whatever the system has
(hunspell packages, usually) and the personal word list is the one other
programs share. The language is the locale's, or the
.B spell-language
key in
.IR ~/.config/gemwrite/settings.ini .
.TP
.B spell-add
Add the misspelled word at the caret to the personal word list. Fails when
the caret is not on a misspelled word.
.TP
.B spell-ignore
Accept the misspelled word at the caret for this run.
.TP
.BI spell-language " LANG"
Check with the dictionary for
.I LANG
(a tag such as en_US; a bare language finds any dictionary for it).
.SS Comments
Comments attach to a range of text and are saved as OpenDocument annotations.
A comment can cover any range, from one word to several paragraphs, so
select just the words it is about: the usual way is
.B find
with the phrase, which selects it, then
.BR comment-add ;
.B select
with two positions covers an exact range. Each command here is one undo
step. Ids come from
.BR comments .
.TP
.BI comment-add " TEXT"
Comment on the selected text; fails without a selection. Select only the
words the comment concerns (see above) rather than the whole paragraph, so
the reader sees what it refers to. Prints the new comment's id. The author is the
.B \-\-author
option if given, else the
.B author
key in
.IR ~/.config/gemwrite/settings.ini ,
else the account's full name, else the login name; the date is now.
.TP
.BI comment-reply " ID TEXT"
Reply to a comment; a reply to a reply joins the same thread. Prints the new
reply's id.
.TP
.BI comment-edit " ID TEXT"
Replace a comment's or reply's text.
.TP
.BI comment-delete " ID"
Remove a comment, its replies, and its mark on the text; or remove one reply.
.TP
.BI comment-select " ID"
Select the text a comment is on.
.SS Query
Queries print one JSON value on standard output and change nothing.
.TP
.B text
The whole document as plain text, paragraphs separated by newlines.
.TP
.B selection
The selected text.
.TP
.B caret
Caret and anchor positions and whether a selection exists.
.TP
.B info
Path, modified flag, paragraph count, word count, and page count.
.TP
.B paragraphs
Every paragraph with its index, style name, alignment, line spacing, left
indent in points, list kind (none, bullet or number) and level, and text.
This is the
easiest way to learn positions before editing.
.TP
.B styles
Paragraph style names available in the document.
.TP
.B attrs
The formatting the next typed character would get: bold, italic, underline,
family, size, paragraph style, alignment, line spacing, left, right and
first-line indents in points, tab stops (position and type), list kind and
level, and the id of the comment on the text at the caret (0 for none).
.TP
.B comments
Every comment in document order, each followed by its replies: id, parent
(0 for a top-level comment), author, date, text, and the start and end
positions of its text. A comment whose text has been deleted is not listed
(undo brings it back).
.TP
.BI render " PNG PAGE"
Paint page number
.I PAGE
(from 0) to a PNG at 144 dpi, using the same layout engine as the window.
.TP
.B misspellings
Every word the dictionary does not know, in order: the word, its start and
end positions, and the dictionary's suggestions, best first.
.TP
.B spell-languages
The language tags with a dictionary installed.
.SH POSITIONS
Positions name a point between characters:
.TP
.B start
The beginning of the document.
.TP
.B end
The end of the document.
.TP
.B caret
Where the caret is now.
.TP
.B anchor
The other end of the selection; equals
.B caret
when nothing is selected.
.TP
.I P
The beginning of paragraph
.IR P ,
counting from 0.
.TP
.IB P : C
Character
.I C
of paragraph
.IR P ,
both counting from 0.
.I C
counts characters, not bytes, so it matches what
.B paragraphs
shows even with non-ASCII text; past the end of the paragraph it means the
end.
.SH UNITS
Units for
.B move
and
.BR extend :
.TP
.B char
One character.
.TP
.B word
To the previous or next word boundary.
.TP
.B line
One visual line up or down, keeping the horizontal position, like the arrow
keys.
.TP
.B line-edge
To the start (negative) or end (positive) of the current visual line, like
Home and End.
.TP
.B page
About one page of lines.
.TP
.B doc
To the start (negative) or end (positive) of the document.
.SH SCRIPT MODE
With
.BR \-\-script ,
lines are read one at a time and each is run as if it had been typed on the
command line. Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are skipped. Words are split with shell quoting rules, so double or single
quotes group arguments containing spaces. A line may hold several commands.
.PP
Query output is flushed after every line, so a program holding both pipes can
write a command, read its reply, and decide what to send next. There is no
prompt.
.PP
An error prints the line number to standard error and stops the run. Lines
after it do not execute; anything already saved stays saved.
.SH OUTPUT
Query commands print JSON, one value per line. Editing commands print nothing
on success. Errors go to standard error prefixed with the command name.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the commands:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gemwrite-cli \-\-list
.RE
.fi
.PP
Create a document from nothing:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gemwrite-cli style-title insert-text "Trip Report" \\
            new-paragraph style-body \\
            insert-text "We saw a lynx near the river." \\
            new-paragraph list-bullet insert-text "Bring boots" \\
            new-paragraph insert-text "Bring a map" \\
            save-as report.odt
.RE
.fi
.PP
See what is in a file before touching it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gemwrite-cli report.odt info
gemwrite-cli report.odt paragraphs
.RE
.fi
.PP
Replace a word everywhere, or just its first occurrence (the
.B find
selects it and
.B insert-text
overwrites the selection):
.PP
.nf
.RS
gemwrite-cli report.odt replace-all lynx bobcat save
gemwrite-cli report.odt find lynx insert-text bobcat save
.RE
.fi
.PP
Make the first seven characters of the first paragraph bold and check the
result:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gemwrite-cli report.odt select 0 0:7 bold attrs save
.RE
.fi
.PP
Append a centred paragraph at the end:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gemwrite-cli report.odt goto end new-paragraph \\
            insert-text "The End" align center save
.RE
.fi
.PP
Turn paragraph 3 into a heading:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gemwrite-cli report.odt goto 3 style-h2 save
.RE
.fi
.PP
Look at the page:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gemwrite-cli report.odt render page0.png 0
.RE
.fi
.PP
Review a document. Each comment goes on just the words it is about:
.B find
selects a phrase (its first occurrence after the caret, so quote enough to
be unique), and
.B select
covers an exact range when the phrase is not distinctive. The last command
lists the comments with their ranges:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gemwrite-cli \-\-author Reviewer report.odt \\
  find "very unique" comment-add "Either unique or not; drop 'very'." \\
  find "the the" comment-add "Doubled word." \\
  select 4:0 4:31 comment-add "This sentence contradicts the opening." \\
  save comments
.RE
.fi
.PP
The whole paragraph is the right range only for a comment about the
paragraph as a whole, such as its place in the argument (an offset past
the end of a paragraph means its end):
.PP
.nf
.RS
gemwrite-cli report.odt select 4 4:9999 comment-add "Move this above the summary." save
.RE
.fi
.PP
The same edits from a script file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
cat > edits.txt <<'END'
# fix the species and add a closing line
find lynx
insert-text bobcat
goto end
new-paragraph
insert-text "Filed by the ranger on duty."
save
END
gemwrite-cli report.odt \-\-script edits.txt
.RE
.fi
.PP
The same from standard input:
.PP
.nf
.RS
printf 'goto end\\nnew-paragraph\\ninsert-text "Addendum"\\nsave\\n' \\
  | gemwrite-cli report.odt \-\-script \-
.RE
.fi
.PP
Batch over several files in one process:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gemwrite-cli \-\-script \- <<'END'
open a.odt
find draft
insert-text final
save
open b.odt
find draft
insert-text final
save
END
.RE
.fi
.PP
Convert plain text to ODT:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gemwrite-cli notes.txt save-as notes.odt
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every command succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
A command failed or the file could not be loaded; the message on standard
error says which.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gemwrite ,
GemWM's README
